// Thinner subjects, part 14 (28 November 2026): Plants and Ecosystems, The Human Body, Arts and Culture, Planet Earth.
ADDART({id:"airplants",t:"Air Plants and Epiphytes",c:"Plants",i:"🌬️",w:"Epiphyte",r:["cloudforests","orchids","rainforests","ferns","pineapples","parasiticplants"],
p:[["Plants that live on plants","Epiphytes grow on trees without harming them."],["No soil needed","They take water and nutrients from rain and air."],["Orchids, ferns and bromeliads","Many famous plants are epiphytes."]],
l:"Epiphytes are plants that grow on other plants, usually on the branches and trunks of trees, without taking food or water from them. Unlike parasites, they simply use their hosts for support, reaching up toward the light in crowded forests. Epiphytes get water from rain, mist and dew, and nutrients from dust, rotting leaves and animal droppings that collect around them. About 10 percent of all vascular plant species, roughly 30,000 species, are epiphytes, most of them in tropical rainforests and cloud forests. Famous epiphytes include many orchids, ferns such as staghorn ferns, mosses, lichens and bromeliads, a family that includes the pineapple. Some tank bromeliads form a cup of leaves that holds water, creating tiny ponds high in the trees where frogs, insects and even crabs live. Air plants, members of the bromeliad genus Tillandsia from the Americas, are especially well adapted: covered in tiny scales called trichomes that absorb water and nutrients, many have only small roots used just to cling on. Spanish moss, which hangs in gray curtains from trees in the southern United States, is a Tillandsia, not a moss. Air plants have become popular houseplants because they need no soil.",
s:[["Not parasites","Epiphytes do not steal from their hosts, although large numbers can sometimes make branches heavy enough to break. In contrast, parasitic plants such as mistletoe tap into their host’s tissues."],["Tank bromeliads","The leaves of tank bromeliads overlap to form a water-holding cup. Poison dart frogs carry their tadpoles to these tiny pools, and scientists have found hundreds of species of small animals living in them."],["Air plants","Tillandsia species grow on trees, rocks, cliffs and even power lines from the southern United States to Argentina. Their trichomes look silvery and can soak up water within minutes after rain."],["Orchids","Around two-thirds of the world’s orchid species are epiphytes. Their thick, spongy roots, covered in a silvery layer called velamen, absorb moisture from humid air."]],
k:[["Epiphytes","Plants that grow on other plants"],["Share of plant species","About 10 percent"],["Water from","Rain, mist and dew"],["Famous examples","Orchids, ferns, bromeliads, mosses"],["Air plants","Genus Tillandsia"],["Spanish moss","An air plant, not a moss"]],
s2:[["Treetop gardens","A single large rainforest tree can carry dozens of species of epiphytes, forming hanging gardens high above the ground."],["Easy houseplants","Air plants can be grown on driftwood or in glass globes and only need regular misting or soaking."]],
g:[["Epiphyte","A plant that grows on another plant without harming it"],["Bromeliad","A plant family that includes pineapples and air plants"],["Trichome","A tiny hair or scale on a plant’s surface"],["Velamen","A spongy layer on orchid roots"]],
f:["Epiphytes grow on trees without harming them.","Spanish moss is not a moss.","Many orchids are epiphytes."],
fun:"Some poison dart frogs raise their tadpoles in tiny ponds held inside bromeliad leaves high in the trees."});

ADDART({id:"tumbleweeds",t:"Tumbleweeds",c:"Plants",i:"🌾",w:"Tumbleweed",r:["seeddispersal","desert","grasslands","invasiveplants","wind","plantgrowth"],
p:[["Rolling plants","Dried plants that break off and roll in the wind."],["Spreading seeds","As they tumble, they scatter thousands of seeds."],["A symbol of the West","Common in films about the American West."]],
l:"A tumbleweed is the dried upper part of certain plants that, once mature and dead, breaks away from its root and is blown along by the wind, scattering seeds as it rolls. Tumbleweeds grow in dry, open places such as steppes, deserts and prairies. The best-known species, Russian thistle (Salsola tragus), is native to the steppes of Eurasia but arrived in the United States in the 1870s, probably mixed in with flax seeds brought by farmers, and spread rapidly across the dry West. A single large Russian thistle can produce up to about 200,000 seeds. Tumbling is a clever way to spread seeds over long distances in flat, windswept landscapes where there is little to block them. Tumbleweeds became a famous symbol of the American West in cowboy films and songs, but they are actually invasive there, and they can be a nuisance: piling up against fences and houses, blocking roads, spreading wildfires and damaging crops. Other tumbling plants exist around the world, including some amaranths, mustards and the steppe grass Spinifex on Australian beaches, whose seed heads roll along the sand.",
s:[["How they break free","As the plant dries in autumn, a layer of cells at the base of the stem weakens, so the wind snaps it off. The round, bushy shape then rolls easily."],["A traveler from Eurasia","Russian thistle first appeared in South Dakota in the 1870s and within about 20 years had reached the Pacific coast, carried by wind, trains and farm equipment."],["Tumbleweed invasions","Strong winds sometimes pile huge numbers of tumbleweeds into towns. In 2020 and 2021, places in the western United States were buried by tumbleweeds, with some homes surrounded up to the rooftops."],["Uses","Young Russian thistle shoots can be eaten by livestock, and in drought years ranchers have used them as emergency feed. Some people make Christmas decorations from them."]],
k:[["What it is","A dried plant that rolls in the wind"],["Best-known species","Russian thistle (Salsola tragus)"],["Native to","Eurasian steppes"],["Arrived in the US","1870s"],["Seeds per plant","Up to about 200,000"],["Problems","Blocks roads, spreads fires"]],
d:[["1870s","Russian thistle arrives in South Dakota"],["1890s","Tumbleweeds reach the Pacific coast"],["1934","The song ‘Tumbling Tumbleweeds’ is released"]],
s2:[["Tumbleweed snowman","Some towns in the American Southwest build ‘snowmen’ out of tumbleweeds at Christmas."],["Wind-powered","Tumbleweeds have been clocked rolling at highway speeds in strong winds."]],
g:[["Tumbleweed","A dry plant that rolls in the wind spreading seeds"],["Steppe","A large, dry grassland"],["Invasive species","A non-native species that causes harm"],["Seed dispersal","The spreading of seeds away from the parent plant"]],
f:["Tumbleweeds spread seeds by rolling.","Russian thistle came from Eurasia.","Tumbleweeds can block roads and spread fires."],
fun:"The tumbleweed, a symbol of the American West, actually arrived from Eurasia in the 1870s."});

ADDART({id:"waterhyacinth",t:"Water Hyacinth",c:"Plants",i:"💐",w:"Pontederia crassipes",r:["invasiveplants","lakes","wetlands","amazon","biodivloss","rivers"],
p:[["A beautiful invader","Purple flowers on floating plants."],["Doubles quickly","It can double in area in about two weeks."],["Chokes lakes and rivers","It blocks boats, fishing and sunlight."]],
l:"Water hyacinth (Pontederia crassipes, formerly Eichhornia crassipes) is a free-floating aquatic plant native to the Amazon basin of South America. It has glossy green leaves, swollen spongy stalks filled with air that keep it afloat, long dangling roots and spikes of beautiful lavender-blue flowers. Because of its flowers, it was spread around the world as an ornamental plant in the late 1800s, but it escaped into lakes and rivers in Africa, Asia, Australia, North America and Europe, becoming one of the world’s worst invasive species. Water hyacinth grows extremely fast, reproducing mainly by sending out runners that form new plants; under good conditions, a mat can double in size in about two weeks. Dense mats can cover entire lakes and rivers, blocking boats, ferries and fishing, clogging water intakes and hydroelectric dams, using up oxygen in the water, killing fish and providing breeding places for mosquitoes and snails that spread disease. Lake Victoria in East Africa was badly infested in the 1990s. Control methods include removing it by hand or machine, herbicides and releasing weevils that eat it. People also turn harvested water hyacinth into furniture, baskets, paper, compost and biogas.",
s:[["Lake Victoria","In the 1990s, water hyacinth covered tens of thousands of hectares of Lake Victoria, cutting off fishing villages and ports in Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania. Releasing weevils from South America, together with mechanical removal, greatly reduced it by the early 2000s, though it has returned at times."],["Why it spreads so fast","In its native Amazon, insects and diseases keep it in check. Elsewhere, without these enemies, and helped by warm temperatures and water polluted with nutrients from farms and sewage, it grows unchecked."],["Biological control","Small weevils of the genus Neochetina, which eat the leaves and stems, have been released in more than 30 countries. They work slowly but can greatly reduce infestations."],["Useful uses","In countries such as Thailand, Indonesia and Kenya, people dry the stalks to weave furniture, bags and baskets. Water hyacinth can also absorb pollutants and is being tested for cleaning wastewater."]],
k:[["Native to","Amazon basin, South America"],["Type","Free-floating aquatic plant"],["Growth","Mats can double in about two weeks"],["Status","One of the world’s worst invasive species"],["Famous infestation","Lake Victoria, 1990s"],["Control","Removal, herbicides, weevils"]],
d:[["1884","Water hyacinth displayed at an exposition in New Orleans"],["1990s","Massive infestation of Lake Victoria"],["2020","Plant moved to the genus Pontederia"]],
s2:[["Floating gardens","Water hyacinth’s air-filled leaf stalks act like life jackets, keeping the plant on the surface."],["Biogas","Rotting water hyacinth can be used to produce biogas, a fuel for cooking."]],
g:[["Aquatic plant","A plant that lives in water"],["Invasive species","A non-native species that causes harm"],["Runner","A stem that grows sideways to form new plants"],["Biological control","Using natural enemies to control a pest"]],
f:["Water hyacinth comes from the Amazon.","It grows very fast.","Weevils help control it."],
fun:"A mat of water hyacinth can double in size in about two weeks."});

ADDART({id:"teaplant",t:"The Tea Plant",c:"Plants",i:"🍵",w:"Camellia sinensis",r:["coffee","chinacountry","indiacountry","herbs","silk","plantgrowth"],
p:[["One plant, many teas","Green, black, white and oolong tea come from the same plant."],["From China","Tea has been drunk for thousands of years."],["The world’s favorite","After water, tea is the most-consumed drink."]],
l:"The tea plant (Camellia sinensis) is an evergreen shrub or small tree whose leaves are used to make tea, the most widely consumed drink in the world after water. It is native to southwestern China and nearby parts of Southeast Asia, and there are two main varieties: the small-leaved Chinese variety and the large-leaved Assam variety from India. Left alone, it can grow into a tree over 10 meters tall, but on tea plantations it is pruned into waist-high bushes so that workers can pick the youngest leaves and buds, traditionally ‘two leaves and a bud’. All true teas come from this one plant; the differences come from how the leaves are processed. Green tea leaves are heated soon after picking to stop them oxidizing; black tea leaves are rolled and allowed to oxidize fully, turning dark; oolong is partly oxidized; and white tea is made from young buds that are simply withered and dried. Herbal ‘teas’, such as chamomile or mint, are made from other plants. China is the world’s largest tea producer, followed by India, Kenya, Sri Lanka and Türkiye. Tea contains caffeine and compounds called catechins.",
s:[["Ancient history","According to Chinese legend, the emperor Shennong discovered tea around 2737 BCE when leaves blew into his boiling water. Archaeologists have found tea in the tomb of a Han dynasty emperor from about 2,100 years ago. Tea spread to Japan, Korea and later along trade routes worldwide."],["Processing","Picking, withering, rolling, oxidizing and drying turn fresh green leaves into tea. The amount of oxidation, a chemical reaction with air like an apple turning brown, gives each type its color and flavor."],["Tea around the world","Tea cultures include Chinese gongfu tea, the Japanese tea ceremony with powdered matcha, British afternoon tea, Moroccan mint tea, Indian masala chai and Russian tea from a samovar."],["Growing tea","Tea grows best in warm, rainy places with acidic soil, often on hillsides in places such as Darjeeling and Assam in India, Yunnan and Fujian in China, the highlands of Kenya and Sri Lanka."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Camellia sinensis"],["Native to","Southwestern China and nearby regions"],["Main varieties","Chinese and Assam"],["Types of tea","Green, black, oolong, white"],["Largest producer","China"],["Picking","Two leaves and a bud"]],
d:[["c. 2737 BCE","Legend of Shennong discovering tea"],["c. 760 CE","Lu Yu writes The Classic of Tea"],["1610","Dutch traders bring tea to Europe"],["1823","Assam tea plants identified in India"],["1773","Boston Tea Party"]],
s2:[["Tea bags","Tea bags became popular in the early 1900s, reportedly after a New York merchant sent samples in small silk bags."],["Caffeine","A cup of tea usually contains less caffeine than a cup of coffee, though it varies by type and brewing time."]],
g:[["Oxidation","A chemical change when leaves react with air"],["Plantation","A large farm growing one crop"],["Matcha","Powdered green tea from Japan"],["Caffeine","A natural stimulant in tea and coffee"]],
f:["Green and black tea come from the same plant.","Tea comes originally from China.","China produces the most tea."],
fun:"Green tea, black tea, oolong and white tea all come from the leaves of the same plant."});

ADDART({id:"sugarcane",t:"Sugarcane",c:"Plants",i:"🎋",w:"Sugarcane",r:["grasslands","bamboo","biofuels","slavetrade","brazilcountry"],
p:[["A giant grass","Its tall stalks are full of sweet juice."],["Most of the world’s sugar","About 80 percent of sugar comes from cane."],["Biofuel too","Brazil runs cars on sugarcane ethanol."]],
l:"Sugarcane is a tall, perennial grass, growing 3 to 6 meters high, with thick, jointed stalks full of sweet juice. It is native to New Guinea, where it was first domesticated about 8,000 to 10,000 years ago, and spread to Southeast Asia, India, the Middle East and the Mediterranean. People in India were making crystallized sugar from cane juice by around 350 CE. Sugarcane grows best in hot, wet tropical and subtropical climates and is now grown in more than 100 countries, producing about 80 percent of the world’s sugar; the rest comes from sugar beet. Brazil is by far the largest producer, followed by India. To make sugar, harvested cane is crushed to extract the juice, which is boiled and crystallized; the dark syrup left over is molasses, used in cooking and to make rum. The leftover fibers, called bagasse, are burned to power sugar mills or made into paper. Sugarcane has a dark history: from the 1500s to the 1800s, European colonists forced millions of enslaved Africans to work on sugar plantations in the Caribbean and the Americas. Today, sugarcane is also used to make ethanol fuel, especially in Brazil.",
s:[["From cane to sugar","Cane is cut by hand or machine and crushed between rollers. The juice is cleaned, boiled into thick syrup and spun in centrifuges to separate sugar crystals from molasses. Raw sugar is brown; refining removes more molasses to make white sugar."],["Sugar and slavery","The demand for sugar in Europe drove the transatlantic slave trade. Plantations in Brazil, the Caribbean and elsewhere relied on enslaved Africans working in brutal conditions."],["Ethanol","Since the 1970s, Brazil has made ethanol from sugarcane to fuel cars. Most new cars in Brazil are ‘flex-fuel’ vehicles that can run on ethanol, gasoline or any mix."],["Health","Sugarcane juice is a popular street drink in many countries. Health authorities recommend limiting added sugars, because eating too much is linked to tooth decay, obesity and diabetes."]],
k:[["Type","Giant perennial grass"],["Height","About 3–6 meters"],["Domesticated","New Guinea, about 8,000–10,000 years ago"],["Share of world sugar","About 80 percent"],["Largest producer","Brazil"],["By-products","Molasses, bagasse, ethanol"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BCE","Sugarcane domesticated in New Guinea"],["c. 350 CE","Crystallized sugar made in India"],["1493","Columbus brings sugarcane to the Caribbean"],["1975","Brazil launches its ethanol program"]],
s2:[["The word sugar","The word sugar comes, through Arabic and Persian, from the Sanskrit word ‘śarkarā’."],["Chewing cane","In many tropical countries, people chew pieces of fresh cane to enjoy the sweet juice."]],
g:[["Perennial","A plant that lives for many years"],["Molasses","Dark syrup left after making sugar"],["Bagasse","Fiber left after crushing cane"],["Ethanol","An alcohol used as fuel"]],
f:["Sugarcane is a giant grass.","Most of the world’s sugar comes from sugarcane.","Brazil is the largest producer."],
fun:"The leftover fibers from sugarcane are burned to power the very mills that crush it."});

ADDART({id:"durian",t:"Durian",c:"Plants",i:"🍈",w:"Durian",r:["fruits","rainforests","bat","malaysiacountry","thailandcountry","indonesiacountry"],
p:[["The king of fruits","Prized in Southeast Asia for its creamy flesh."],["A famous smell","Its strong odor gets it banned on some trains."],["Spiky giant","The fruit can weigh up to about 3 kilograms."]],
l:"The durian is a large, spiky tropical fruit native to the rainforests of Borneo, Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula, and grown widely in Southeast Asia. Known as the ‘king of fruits’, it can grow up to about 30 centimeters long and weigh 1 to 3 kilograms, with a hard, green to brown husk covered in thick, sharp spines. Inside are pods of soft, creamy, custard-like flesh, which may be yellow, white or orange. Durian is famous for two things: a rich, sweet flavor that fans describe as like almonds, caramel and cheese combined, and a powerful smell that others compare to rotting onions, turpentine or old socks. Because of its strong odor, durians are banned on public transport and in many hotels in places such as Singapore. The smell comes from dozens of chemical compounds, including sulfur compounds. Durian trees can grow over 40 meters tall, and their flowers open at night and are pollinated mainly by bats. There are hundreds of varieties; famous ones include Musang King from Malaysia and Monthong from Thailand. Durian is eaten fresh or used in sweets, ice cream, cakes and savory dishes, and demand from China has grown enormously in recent years.",
s:[["The smell","Scientists have identified more than 40 aromatic compounds in durian, including some that smell fruity and others that smell like onion or rotten egg. The fruit’s scent attracts animals that eat it and spread its seeds."],["Bats as pollinators","Durian flowers open in the evening and are pollinated by fruit bats such as the cave nectar bat. Protecting bats and their caves is important for durian growers."],["Love it or hate it","The naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating durian was ‘a new sensation, worth a voyage to the East to experience’. Others find the smell unbearable."],["Booming trade","Durian exports from Thailand, Malaysia and Vietnam to China have boomed, with China becoming the world’s largest durian market in the 2020s."]],
k:[["Native to","Borneo, Sumatra, Malay Peninsula"],["Nickname","King of fruits"],["Weight","About 1–3 kg"],["Famous for","Strong smell and creamy flesh"],["Pollinators","Mainly bats"],["Famous varieties","Musang King, Monthong"]],
d:[["1856","Alfred Russel Wallace praises durian"],["2017","Durian genome sequenced"]],
s2:[["Warning signs","Signs in Singapore’s subway show a durian with a red line through it, next to signs banning smoking and flammable goods."],["Falling fruit","Durians fall from tall trees when ripe, so people working in orchards sometimes wear helmets."]],
g:[["Husk","The hard outer covering of a fruit"],["Aroma","A strong smell"],["Pollinator","An animal that carries pollen between flowers"],["Variety","A type of plant within a species"]],
f:["Durian is called the king of fruits.","Durian has a very strong smell.","Bats pollinate durian flowers."],
fun:"Durians are banned on Singapore’s subway trains because of their smell."});

ADDART({id:"woundhealing",t:"Cuts, Bruises and Healing",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩹",w:"Wound healing",r:["skin","bloodclotting","immune","stemcells","firstaid","vitamins"],
p:[["The body repairs itself","Skin and tissues heal in stages."],["Bruises change color","From purple to green to yellow."],["Scars","New tissue fills deeper wounds."]],
l:"When the skin is cut, scraped or bruised, the body starts a remarkable repair process called wound healing. It happens in overlapping stages. First, bleeding is stopped by blood clotting, as platelets form a plug and threads of fibrin create a clot, which dries into a scab on the surface. Second, during inflammation, which lasts a few days, the area becomes red, warm and swollen as white blood cells arrive to fight germs and clear away damaged cells. Third, in the rebuilding stage, new blood vessels grow and cells called fibroblasts produce collagen, a strong protein that fills the wound, while skin cells crawl across from the edges to cover it. Finally, over months, the new tissue is strengthened and reorganized. Small cuts heal without a trace, but deeper wounds leave scars, made of collagen that is less flexible than normal skin. A bruise happens when a knock breaks tiny blood vessels under the skin without breaking the surface; blood leaks into the tissue and is gradually broken down, changing color from red or purple to blue, green and yellow over about two weeks. Keeping wounds clean and covered helps them heal well.",
s:[["Stages of healing","Doctors describe four stages: hemostasis (stopping bleeding), inflammation, proliferation (rebuilding) and remodeling. Small scrapes may heal in a week, while deep wounds can take months to reach full strength."],["Bruise colors","Hemoglobin, the red pigment in blood, breaks down into biliverdin, which is green, and then bilirubin, which is yellow. That is why bruises change color as they heal."],["Scars","Scars form when the deeper layer of skin, the dermis, is damaged. Some people develop raised scars called keloids. Scars usually fade and flatten over a year or more."],["Helping healing","Clean small wounds with water, apply gentle pressure to stop bleeding, and cover them with a clean dressing. Seek medical help for deep, gaping or dirty wounds, animal bites, or signs of infection such as spreading redness, pus or fever."]],
k:[["Stages","Stop bleeding, inflammation, rebuilding, remodeling"],["Scab made of","Dried clot"],["Rebuilding protein","Collagen"],["Bruise","Blood leaking under unbroken skin"],["Bruise colors","Purple, blue, green, yellow"],["Scars form when","The deeper skin layer is damaged"]],
s2:[["Faster in the mouth","Wounds inside the mouth heal faster and with less scarring than skin wounds, partly thanks to saliva and a rich blood supply."],["Moist healing","Research shows that keeping a wound slightly moist under a dressing often helps it heal faster than letting it dry out."]],
g:[["Inflammation","Redness, warmth and swelling as the body responds to injury"],["Collagen","A strong protein in skin and connective tissue"],["Fibroblast","A cell that makes collagen"],["Keloid","A raised, overgrown scar"]],
f:["Wound healing happens in stages.","Bruises change color as they heal.","Scars are made of collagen."],
fun:"A bruise changes color as the body breaks down blood pigments into green and yellow chemicals."});

ADDART({id:"skinmarks",t:"Freckles, Moles and Birthmarks",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🔵",w:"Mole (skin)",r:["skin","mendel","cancer","hair","woundhealing","bodytemperature"],
p:[["Spots on the skin","Freckles, moles and birthmarks are common."],["Made by pigment","Melanin gives them their color."],["Watch for changes","Changing moles should be checked."]],
l:"Freckles, moles and birthmarks are common marks on the skin, mostly harmless. Freckles are small, flat, light brown spots caused by extra melanin, the pigment that colors skin, hair and eyes. They are most common in people with fair skin and red or light hair, run in families and become darker and more numerous with sun exposure, often fading in winter. Moles, called nevi by doctors, are small growths made of clusters of pigment cells called melanocytes. They are usually brown or black, round or oval, and can be flat or raised. Most people have between 10 and 40 moles, many appearing during childhood and the teenage years. Birthmarks are marks present at birth or appearing soon after; they include pigmented birthmarks such as café-au-lait spots and congenital moles, and vascular birthmarks caused by blood vessels, such as strawberry marks, which often fade during childhood, and port-wine stains. Most skin marks need no treatment, but because melanoma, a serious skin cancer, can develop in or look like a mole, doctors recommend checking moles for changes in size, shape or color and protecting skin from the sun.",
s:[["Freckles","Freckles are linked to variations in a gene called MC1R, which also affects red hair. Sun exposure triggers melanocytes to make more melanin in these spots, which is why freckles darken in summer."],["Checking moles: ABCDE","Doctors suggest watching for Asymmetry, irregular Borders, uneven Color, a Diameter larger than about 6 millimeters, and Evolving, or changing, moles. Any changing or unusual mole should be checked by a doctor."],["Strawberry marks","Infantile hemangiomas, or strawberry marks, are raised red birthmarks made of extra blood vessels. They often grow during the first months of life and then slowly shrink, with many fading by age 10."],["Sun protection","Sunscreen, hats, shade and avoiding sunburn, especially in childhood, reduce the number of new moles and the risk of skin cancer."]],
k:[["Freckles","Flat spots of extra melanin"],["Moles","Clusters of pigment cells (nevi)"],["Typical number of moles","About 10–40"],["Birthmark types","Pigmented and vascular"],["Mole check","ABCDE rule"],["Pigment","Melanin"]],
s2:[["Beauty marks","Through history, moles on the face have sometimes been considered beautiful, called beauty marks, and people even painted on fake ones."],["Ephelides","The scientific name for freckles is ephelides."]],
g:[["Melanin","The pigment that colors skin, hair and eyes"],["Melanocyte","A cell that makes melanin"],["Nevus","The medical name for a mole"],["Melanoma","A serious type of skin cancer"]],
f:["Freckles darken in the sun.","Moles are clusters of pigment cells.","Changing moles should be checked by a doctor."],
fun:"Freckles often fade in winter and return in summer."});

ADDART({id:"urinarysystem",t:"The Urinary System",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🚽",w:"Urinary system",r:["kidney","hydration","blood","digest","hormone","metabolism"],
p:[["Cleaning the blood","Kidneys filter waste into urine."],["Storage tank","The bladder holds urine until it is time to go."],["About 1.5 liters a day","Adults make roughly this much urine daily."]],
l:"The urinary system removes waste and extra water from the body and helps keep its chemistry in balance. It is made up of two kidneys, two ureters, the bladder and the urethra. The kidneys, bean-shaped organs about the size of a fist, filter the entire blood supply many times a day, removing waste products such as urea, which comes from breaking down protein, along with excess salts and water, to make urine. Each kidney contains about a million tiny filtering units called nephrons. Urine flows from each kidney down a narrow tube called a ureter into the bladder, a stretchy, muscular bag in the lower abdomen. When the bladder fills, typically holding around 400 to 600 milliliters in adults, stretch sensors send signals to the brain, creating the urge to urinate. When a person decides to go, muscles relax and the bladder squeezes urine out through the urethra. Adults usually produce about 1 to 2 liters of urine a day. The color of urine, from pale yellow to dark amber, gives a clue about how well hydrated someone is. Common problems include urinary tract infections, kidney stones and incontinence.",
s:[["Kidneys at work","About a fifth of the blood pumped by the heart passes through the kidneys. They filter about 180 liters of fluid a day but return almost all of it to the blood, leaving only 1 to 2 liters as urine."],["Balancing the body","Besides removing waste, the kidneys control the amount of water and salts in the body, help regulate blood pressure, make a hormone that tells bone marrow to produce red blood cells, and activate vitamin D."],["Common problems","Urinary tract infections, caused by bacteria, often cause a burning feeling when urinating and are treated with antibiotics. Kidney stones are hard crystals that can cause severe pain. Drinking enough water helps prevent both."],["Kidney failure","If kidneys stop working, waste builds up in the blood. Treatment includes dialysis, in which a machine filters the blood, or a kidney transplant. A person can live a healthy life with just one kidney."]],
k:[["Parts","Kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra"],["Filtering units","About 1 million nephrons per kidney"],["Fluid filtered","About 180 liters a day"],["Urine made","About 1–2 liters a day"],["Bladder capacity","About 400–600 ml (adults)"],["Key waste","Urea"]],
d:[["1943","First artificial kidney dialysis machine, by Willem Kolff"],["1954","First successful kidney transplant, in Boston"]],
s2:[["Color clue","Pale straw-colored urine usually means good hydration; dark urine can mean you need to drink more water."],["Space urine","On the International Space Station, astronauts’ urine is recycled into clean drinking water."]],
g:[["Kidney","An organ that filters blood"],["Ureter","A tube carrying urine from a kidney to the bladder"],["Bladder","A muscular bag that stores urine"],["Nephron","A tiny filtering unit in the kidney"]],
f:["The kidneys filter blood.","Urine is stored in the bladder.","Urine color shows hydration."],
fun:"Astronauts on the International Space Station recycle their urine into drinking water."});

ADDART({id:"heightgrowth",t:"How We Grow Taller",c:"Health and the Body",i:"📏",w:"Human height",r:["bones","puberty","hormone","nutri","sleep","mendel"],
p:[["Growth plates","Bones grow at their ends during childhood."],["Genes and food","Height depends on genes, nutrition and health."],["Growth stops","Most people reach adult height in their late teens."]],
l:"Children grow taller because their bones get longer. Long bones, such as those in the legs and arms, have areas near their ends called growth plates, made of cartilage. Cells in the growth plates multiply and are gradually turned into bone, lengthening the bone. Growth is fastest in the first year of life, when babies grow about 25 centimeters, slows during childhood to about 5 to 6 centimeters a year, then speeds up again in a growth spurt during puberty, before stopping when the growth plates close and harden, usually by about age 14 to 16 in girls and 16 to 18 in boys. Growth is controlled by hormones, especially growth hormone, released by the pituitary gland mostly during deep sleep, and by thyroid and sex hormones. How tall a person becomes depends mostly on their genes, with hundreds of genes involved, but also on nutrition, health and living conditions during childhood. Average heights have increased greatly in many countries over the past 150 years as diets and health care improved: Dutch men, among the tallest, average over 1.8 meters. People are slightly taller in the morning than in the evening, because the discs in the spine compress during the day.",
s:[["Growth plates","Doctors can estimate a child’s ‘bone age’ by looking at the growth plates on an X-ray of the hand. Once the plates have fused into solid bone, a person cannot grow taller."],["Genes and environment","Studies of twins suggest about 80 percent of differences in height between people in a population are due to genes. But poor nutrition and frequent illness in childhood can prevent children reaching their genetic potential."],["Getting taller over time","A study of 200 countries found that average heights rose dramatically over the 20th century. South Korean women gained about 20 centimeters, and Iranian men about 16 centimeters, on average."],["Morning and evening","During the day, gravity squeezes the soft discs between the vertebrae, so people can be about 1 centimeter shorter in the evening than when they wake up. Astronauts in space grow a few centimeters taller temporarily."]],
k:[["Growth happens at","Growth plates near bone ends"],["Fastest growth","First year of life (about 25 cm)"],["Key hormone","Growth hormone"],["Growth stops","When growth plates close (late teens)"],["Main influences","Genes, nutrition, health"],["Tallest nations","Netherlands, Montenegro and others"]],
d:[["1956","Growth hormone first isolated from the human pituitary"],["1985","Lab-made growth hormone approved"],["2016","Global study of a century of height changes published"]],
s2:[["Space growth","Astronauts can grow up to about 3 percent taller in space because their spines stretch without gravity, but they shrink back on Earth."],["Sleep and growth","Much growth hormone is released during deep sleep, one reason sleep is important for growing children."]],
g:[["Growth plate","The area of cartilage where a bone grows"],["Growth hormone","A hormone that stimulates growth"],["Cartilage","Firm, flexible tissue"],["Vertebra","A bone of the spine"]],
f:["Bones grow at growth plates.","Height depends mostly on genes.","People are slightly taller in the morning."],
fun:"You are about a centimeter taller when you wake up than when you go to bed."});

ADDART({id:"neuroplasticity",t:"Neuroplasticity",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🧠",w:"Neuroplasticity",r:["brain","memory","nervous","sleep","stroke","langevo"],
p:[["The changing brain","The brain rewires itself as we learn."],["Use it or lose it","Practiced skills strengthen brain connections."],["Recovery","The brain can adapt after injury."]],
l:"Neuroplasticity, or brain plasticity, is the brain’s ability to change its structure and function throughout life in response to experience, learning and injury. The brain contains about 86 billion nerve cells, or neurons, connected by trillions of junctions called synapses. When we learn something new, such as a word, a skill or a fact, connections between certain neurons become stronger, new connections form and unused ones weaken. This is often summarized as ‘neurons that fire together wire together’. Plasticity is greatest in childhood, which is why children learn languages and skills quickly, but it continues in adults. Brain scans have shown that London taxi drivers who memorized the city’s complex streets developed a larger hippocampus, a region involved in navigation and memory, and that learning to juggle can increase gray matter in areas that process movement. Plasticity also helps people recover after strokes or brain injuries, as other brain areas take over lost functions with therapy and practice. Sleep plays an important role in strengthening new memories. Scientists once thought the adult brain could not make new neurons, but research shows a few areas can.",
s:[["Learning and practice","Practicing a skill strengthens the pathways used, making it faster and more automatic. Musicians, athletes and people who speak several languages show differences in brain regions related to their skills."],["The taxi driver study","In 2000, Eleanor Maguire and colleagues at University College London found that taxi drivers who had learned ‘The Knowledge’, London’s street layout, had a larger rear hippocampus than other people."],["Recovery after injury","After a stroke, patients relearn movement and speech through intensive therapy. Healthy areas of the brain can take over some functions of damaged ones, especially with early, repeated practice."],["Critical periods","Some abilities develop best during sensitive periods in early childhood. For example, if a child’s eye is not used properly in early years, vision may not develop normally, which is why lazy eye is treated early."]],
k:[["Definition","The brain’s ability to change"],["Neurons in the brain","About 86 billion"],["Connections","Synapses"],["Greatest in","Childhood"],["Famous study","London taxi drivers (2000)"],["Helps with","Learning and recovery after injury"]],
d:[["1949","Donald Hebb proposes that learning strengthens connections"],["1998","New neurons found in adult human brains"],["2000","Taxi driver hippocampus study published"]],
s2:[["Phantom limbs","After losing a limb, some people feel sensations in it because brain areas that controlled it reorganize."],["Never too late","Older adults can still learn new languages, instruments and skills, though it may take more practice."]],
g:[["Neuroplasticity","The brain’s ability to change"],["Neuron","A nerve cell"],["Synapse","A junction between neurons"],["Hippocampus","A brain area involved in memory and navigation"]],
f:["The brain changes as we learn.","Plasticity is greatest in childhood.","The brain can adapt after injury."],
fun:"London taxi drivers who memorized the city’s streets developed a larger hippocampus."});

ADDART({id:"motionsickness",t:"Motion Sickness",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤢",w:"Motion sickness",r:["balance","ear","brain","eye","ships","spacemedicine"],
p:[["Feeling sick while moving","In cars, boats, planes or virtual reality."],["Mixed signals","Eyes and inner ear disagree about movement."],["Ways to help","Look at the horizon and get fresh air."]],
l:"Motion sickness is the queasy, unwell feeling some people get when traveling in a car, boat, plane or train, riding a swing or roller coaster, or using virtual reality headsets. Symptoms include nausea, dizziness, pale skin, cold sweats, yawning, headache and sometimes vomiting. It is thought to be caused by a mismatch between signals reaching the brain from different senses. The balance organs in the inner ear sense movement and changes of speed and direction, while the eyes report what they see and muscles and joints report the body’s position. When reading in a moving car, for example, the inner ear feels the car turning and bumping, but the eyes, fixed on a still page, report no movement. The brain’s confusion triggers sickness, possibly because such mismatches can also be caused by poisons, and vomiting would help get rid of them. Children aged about 2 to 12, pregnant women and people who get migraines are especially prone. Ways to reduce it include sitting where motion is least, such as the front seat of a car or the middle of a ship, looking at the horizon, avoiding reading or screens, getting fresh air, and medicines or wristbands.",
s:[["Seasickness","On ships, rolling waves move the body in many directions. Seasickness is common on small boats; most people adapt after a few days at sea, called getting your ‘sea legs’."],["Space sickness","About half of astronauts feel space motion sickness in their first days in weightlessness, as the inner ear no longer senses gravity normally."],["Virtual reality","In VR, the eyes see movement while the body stays still, the opposite of reading in a car. Developers reduce ‘cybersickness’ with steady frame rates and fixed reference points."],["Treatments","Antihistamine medicines such as dimenhydrinate and scopolamine patches can prevent motion sickness. Ginger may help some people. Acupressure wristbands are popular, though evidence for them is mixed."]],
k:[["Cause","Mismatch between eyes and inner ear"],["Symptoms","Nausea, dizziness, sweating, vomiting"],["Common in","Cars, boats, planes, VR"],["Most prone","Children 2–12, pregnant women, migraine sufferers"],["Tips","Look at the horizon, avoid reading, fresh air"],["Medicines","Antihistamines, scopolamine"]],
s2:[["Driver advantage","Drivers rarely get car sick, because they can predict the car’s movements."],["Nausea word","The word nausea comes from the Greek word ‘naus’, meaning ship."]],
g:[["Motion sickness","Feeling sick due to movement"],["Inner ear","The part of the ear that senses balance"],["Nausea","A feeling of wanting to vomit"],["Cybersickness","Motion sickness from virtual reality"]],
f:["Motion sickness comes from mixed signals.","Looking at the horizon can help.","Drivers rarely get car sick."],
fun:"The word ‘nausea’ comes from the Greek word for ship."});

ADDART({id:"flamenco",t:"Flamenco",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💃",w:"Flamenco",r:["worlddance","spaincountry","guitar","folkmusic","tango"],
p:[["Song, guitar and dance","Flamenco combines singing, playing and dancing."],["From Andalusia","It developed in southern Spain."],["UNESCO heritage","Listed as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2010."]],
l:"Flamenco is an expressive art form from Andalusia in southern Spain that combines singing, called cante, guitar playing, called toque, dance, called baile, and rhythmic hand clapping, called palmas. It developed over centuries from the mix of cultures in Andalusia, including Romani (Gitano) people, Andalusian folk traditions, and influences from Moorish, Jewish and other musical cultures, and took recognizable shape in the late 1700s and 1800s. Flamenco is known for its intense emotion, from deep sorrow to joyous celebration. The singing can be raw and passionate, the guitar fast and intricate, and dancers combine proud posture, graceful arm and hand movements and rapid, percussive footwork called zapateado. Flamenco has many styles, called palos, each with its own rhythm, mood and themes, such as the serious siguiriyas and soleá or the lively alegrías and bulerías. In the 1800s, flamenco was performed in cafés cantantes, and in the 20th century artists such as guitarist Paco de Lucía and singer Camarón de la Isla brought it worldwide fame. In 2010 UNESCO added flamenco to its list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.",
s:[["The elements","Cante (song) is often considered the heart of flamenco. The toque (guitar) accompanies and answers the singer. Baile (dance) expresses the music through movement, while palmas and shouts of encouragement, called jaleo, drive the rhythm."],["Palos","There are dozens of palos. Many use a 12-beat rhythm with accents in unusual places, which gives flamenco its distinctive drive."],["Famous artists","Paco de Lucía transformed flamenco guitar from the 1960s, mixing in jazz and other styles. Camarón de la Isla is regarded as one of the greatest flamenco singers. Dancers such as Carmen Amaya and Joaquín Cortés became international stars."],["Flamenco today","Flamenco is taught and performed around the world, with especially large followings in Japan. Modern ‘nuevo flamenco’ mixes it with pop, rock and other genres."]],
k:[["Origin","Andalusia, southern Spain"],["Elements","Song, guitar, dance, hand clapping"],["Roots","Romani, Andalusian, Moorish, Jewish influences"],["Styles","Palos"],["Famous guitarist","Paco de Lucía"],["UNESCO heritage","2010"]],
d:[["1774","Earliest known written description of flamenco-like performance"],["1850s–1920s","Era of the cafés cantantes"],["1922","Cante Jondo competition in Granada"],["2010","Added to UNESCO’s heritage list"]],
s2:[["Japan","Japan reportedly has more flamenco schools than Spain."],["Footwork","Flamenco dancers wear shoes with nails in the heels and toes to make their footwork ring out."]],
g:[["Cante","Flamenco singing"],["Toque","Flamenco guitar playing"],["Baile","Flamenco dance"],["Palo","A style of flamenco"]],
f:["Flamenco comes from Andalusia in Spain.","Flamenco combines song, guitar and dance.","Flamenco is on UNESCO’s heritage list."],
fun:"Japan is said to have more flamenco schools than Spain itself."});

ADDART({id:"tango",t:"Tango",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🌹",w:"Tango",r:["worlddance","argentinacountry","uruguaycountry","flamenco","musicals","folkmusic"],
p:[["A dance of the Río de la Plata","Born in Buenos Aires and Montevideo."],["Close embrace","Partners dance in a close hold."],["Music too","Tango music features the bandoneón."]],
l:"Tango is a partner dance and a style of music that originated in the late 1800s in the working-class neighborhoods of Buenos Aires, Argentina, and Montevideo, Uruguay, along the Río de la Plata. It grew from a mix of influences brought by European immigrants, especially from Italy and Spain, African Argentine and Afro-Uruguayan rhythms such as candombe, Cuban habanera and local milonga music. At first considered rough and improper, tango became a craze in Paris and other European cities around 1913, then returned to Argentina to become respectable and wildly popular. In tango, partners dance in an embrace, sometimes very close, improvising elegant walking steps, pauses, turns and leg movements such as quick kicks called ganchos, with the leader and follower communicating through the embrace. Tango music is often played by an orchestra featuring the bandoneón, a type of concertina from Germany, along with violins, piano and double bass. Famous figures include the singer Carlos Gardel and the composer Astor Piazzolla, who created ‘nuevo tango’. In 2009, UNESCO recognized tango as Intangible Cultural Heritage, nominated jointly by Argentina and Uruguay.",
s:[["Golden age","The 1930s to 1950s are called the golden age of tango, when large orchestras led by musicians such as Juan D’Arienzo, Aníbal Troilo and Osvaldo Pugliese played for huge dances in Buenos Aires."],["Carlos Gardel","Carlos Gardel, the most famous tango singer, starred in films and recorded classics such as ‘Por una cabeza’ and ‘Mi Buenos Aires querido’ before dying in a plane crash in 1935."],["Astor Piazzolla","Piazzolla, a bandoneón player and composer, blended tango with jazz and classical music from the 1950s. His ‘Libertango’ and ‘Adiós Nonino’ are played worldwide."],["Milongas","Social tango dances are called milongas. Dancers follow traditions such as the cabeceo, an invitation to dance made by eye contact and a nod."]],
k:[["Origin","Buenos Aires and Montevideo, late 1800s"],["Influences","European, African, Cuban, local"],["Key instrument","Bandoneón"],["Famous singer","Carlos Gardel"],["Famous composer","Astor Piazzolla"],["UNESCO heritage","2009"]],
d:[["1880s","Tango emerges in Buenos Aires and Montevideo"],["1913","Tango craze in Paris"],["1917","Carlos Gardel records ‘Mi noche triste’"],["1930s–1950s","Golden age of tango"],["2009","Added to UNESCO’s heritage list"]],
s2:[["World championship","Buenos Aires hosts the Tango World Championship each year, drawing dancers from dozens of countries."],["Bandoneón","The bandoneón was invented in Germany for church music before becoming the voice of tango."]],
g:[["Tango","A partner dance and music from the Río de la Plata"],["Bandoneón","A square concertina used in tango"],["Milonga","A tango dance event, or a related lively music style"],["Embrace","The hold between dance partners"]],
f:["Tango began in Buenos Aires and Montevideo.","The bandoneón is a key tango instrument.","Tango is on UNESCO’s heritage list."],
fun:"The bandoneón, the heart of tango music, was originally made in Germany to play hymns."});

ADDART({id:"breakdancing",t:"Breaking (Breakdancing)",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🕺",w:"Breakdancing",r:["hiphop","worlddance","olymp","streetart","rockpop"],
p:[["Born in the Bronx","Breaking began in New York in the 1970s."],["Spins and freezes","Dancers spin on heads, backs and hands."],["Olympic sport","Breaking debuted at the Paris 2024 Olympics."]],
l:"Breaking, often called breakdancing, is an athletic street dance that developed in the 1970s in the Bronx, New York City, among African American and Puerto Rican young people, as part of the hip hop culture that also includes DJing, rapping and graffiti. It began at parties where DJs such as DJ Kool Herc played and repeated the ‘break’, the instrumental drum section of funk records, and dancers, called b-boys and b-girls, showed off their moves. Breaking combines four main elements: toprock, dancing while standing; footwork, fast steps performed low to the ground with hands supporting the body; power moves, acrobatic spins such as windmills, headspins and flares; and freezes, holding a striking pose. Breakers often compete in ‘battles’, taking turns to outdo each other with creativity, musicality and skill. Breaking spread worldwide in the 1980s through films and music videos and is now popular from South Korea and Japan to France, Russia and Brazil. It made its Olympic debut at the Paris 2024 Games, where Ami Yuasa of Japan won the women’s gold medal and Phil Wizard (Philip Kim) of Canada won the men’s gold, though it was not included in the program for Los Angeles 2028.",
s:[["Origins","At a 1973 back-to-school party in the Bronx, DJ Kool Herc used two copies of a record to extend the drum breaks, giving dancers more time to show off. Groups such as the Rock Steady Crew helped develop and popularize the dance."],["Moves","Toprock opens a dancer’s set. Footwork includes the ‘six-step’. Power moves include windmills, where a dancer spins on the back and shoulders with legs in a V, and headspins. A freeze ends a move in a dramatic pose."],["Battles","In battles, breakers or crews take turns. Judges look at technique, creativity, musicality, variety and personality. Major events include the Red Bull BC One and Battle of the Year."],["Olympics","Breaking was added to the Paris 2024 Olympics after appearing at the 2018 Youth Olympic Games in Buenos Aires. It was not chosen for Los Angeles 2028."]],
k:[["Origin","The Bronx, New York, 1970s"],["Part of","Hip hop culture"],["Dancers","B-boys and b-girls"],["Elements","Toprock, footwork, power moves, freezes"],["Olympic debut","Paris 2024"],["Paris 2024 champions","Ami Yuasa and Phil Wizard"]],
d:[["1973","DJ Kool Herc’s party in the Bronx"],["1977","Rock Steady Crew founded"],["1984","Breakdancing films spread it worldwide"],["2018","Breaking at the Youth Olympic Games"],["2024","Breaking at the Paris Olympics"]],
s2:[["The name","The word ‘breaking’ comes from dancing to the break in a song. Many dancers prefer ‘breaking’ to ‘breakdancing’, a term popularized by media."],["Strength and flexibility","Top breakers train like gymnasts, building strength, balance and flexibility to perform power moves safely."]],
g:[["Breaking","A street dance from hip hop culture"],["B-boy / b-girl","A male or female breaker"],["Power move","An acrobatic spinning move"],["Battle","A dance competition between dancers or crews"]],
f:["Breaking began in the Bronx.","Breaking is part of hip hop culture.","Breaking debuted at the Paris 2024 Olympics."],
fun:"Breaking is named after the ‘break’, the drum section of a song that DJs repeated for dancers."});

ADDART({id:"jewelry",t:"Jewelry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💍",w:"Jewellery",r:["gems","clothing","egypt","silk","ceramics"],
p:[["One of the oldest arts","People have worn jewelry for over 100,000 years."],["Many meanings","Beauty, wealth, status, faith and love."],["Metals and gems","Gold, silver, gemstones, shells and beads."]],
l:"Jewelry is decorative items worn on the body, such as necklaces, rings, bracelets, earrings, brooches, anklets and crowns. It is one of the oldest forms of art: shell beads with holes for stringing, found in Morocco, Algeria, Israel and South Africa, are over 100,000 years old. Through history, jewelry has been made from almost every material, including shells, bones, teeth, stones, feathers, seeds, glass, gold, silver, copper, platinum, pearls and gemstones such as diamonds, rubies, sapphires and emeralds. Jewelry has served many purposes: decoration, displaying wealth and status, showing membership of a group, religious or protective meanings, marking life events such as marriage, and even carrying money. Ancient Egyptians wore gold collars, amulets and scarab rings; Tutankhamun’s tomb contained spectacular examples. Many cultures have distinctive traditions, such as Indian gold wedding jewelry, Maasai beadwork in East Africa, Navajo turquoise and silver jewelry in the southwestern United States and Chinese jade. Jewelers use techniques such as casting, soldering, engraving, enameling and stone setting. Famous jewelry houses include Cartier, Tiffany and Bulgari, and some royal jewels, such as the British Crown Jewels, are national treasures.",
s:[["Ancient beads","Shell beads from Bizmoune Cave in Morocco, described in 2021, are about 142,000 to 150,000 years old, among the oldest known. They suggest early humans used ornaments to communicate identity."],["Gold","Gold is ideal for jewelry because it never tarnishes and is easily shaped. The Varna Necropolis in Bulgaria contains gold objects more than 6,000 years old, among the oldest worked gold in the world."],["Traditions","In India, gold jewelry is an important part of weddings and family savings. Maasai women make colorful beaded collars whose colors carry meanings. In many cultures, wedding rings symbolize marriage."],["Techniques","Lost-wax casting, used for thousands of years, involves making a wax model, covering it in clay, melting out the wax and pouring metal into the space. Filigree uses fine twisted wires; granulation uses tiny metal balls."]],
k:[["Oldest known","Shell beads over 100,000 years old"],["Common materials","Gold, silver, gems, pearls, beads"],["Purposes","Beauty, status, faith, marriage, protection"],["Ancient example","Tutankhamun’s treasures"],["Techniques","Casting, engraving, enameling, setting"],["Famous collection","British Crown Jewels"]],
d:[["c. 150,000 years ago","Shell beads in Morocco"],["c. 4500 BCE","Gold objects at Varna, Bulgaria"],["1922","Tutankhamun’s tomb and jewelry discovered"],["1837","Tiffany & Co. founded in New York"]],
s2:[["Carats and karats","Gem weight is measured in carats (1 carat = 0.2 grams), while gold purity is measured in karats, with 24-karat gold being pure."],["Charm bracelets","Charm bracelets, with small pendants marking memories, were popular in ancient times and again in the 20th century."]],
g:[["Jewelry","Decorative items worn on the body"],["Gemstone","A cut and polished mineral used in jewelry"],["Amulet","An object believed to protect its wearer"],["Filigree","Delicate decoration made of fine wire"]],
f:["Jewelry is one of the oldest arts.","Gold does not tarnish.","Some shell beads are over 100,000 years old."],
fun:"The oldest known jewelry, shell beads from Morocco, is about 150,000 years old."});

ADDART({id:"mariachi",t:"Mariachi",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎺",w:"Mariachi",r:["mexicocountry","folkmusic","guitar","countrymusic","worlddance"],
p:[["Mexico’s musical icon","Bands of violins, trumpets and guitars."],["Charro suits","Musicians wear decorated suits and sombreros."],["UNESCO heritage","Listed in 2011."]],
l:"Mariachi is a style of traditional Mexican music and the name of the musical groups that perform it. It originated in western Mexico, especially the state of Jalisco and its capital Guadalajara, in the 1700s and 1800s, growing from rural folk music that blended Spanish, Indigenous and African influences. A modern mariachi band usually has three to a dozen or more musicians playing violins, trumpets, a Spanish guitar, a vihuela (a small, high-pitched guitar with a rounded back) and a guitarrón (a large acoustic bass guitar), with many members also singing. Musicians traditionally wear the traje de charro, a tight, decorated suit inspired by Mexican horsemen, with a wide-brimmed sombrero. Mariachi songs cover love, betrayal, death, heroes, animals, landscapes and pride in Mexico, and include styles such as the son jalisciense, ranchera, bolero and huapango. Mariachi bands play at weddings, birthdays, serenades, religious festivals and celebrations, and became internationally famous through Mexican films and radio from the 1930s. ‘Cielito Lindo’ and ‘El Son de la Negra’ are among the best-known songs. In 2011, UNESCO added mariachi to its list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.",
s:[["Instruments","Trumpets were added to mariachi bands in the 1930s and 1940s, giving the bright sound many people know today. The vihuela and guitarrón provide rhythm and bass."],["Serenades","It is a tradition to hire mariachis to serenade someone, such as a loved one on their birthday or Mother’s Day, often early in the morning with the song ‘Las Mañanitas’."],["Plaza Garibaldi","In Mexico City, mariachi bands gather in Plaza Garibaldi, where people can hire them to play songs on the spot."],["Around the world","Mariachi is popular across Latin America and in the United States, and there are mariachi groups in places as far away as Japan and Croatia. Guadalajara hosts an international mariachi festival each year."]],
k:[["Origin","Jalisco, western Mexico"],["Instruments","Violins, trumpets, guitar, vihuela, guitarrón"],["Costume","Traje de charro and sombrero"],["Styles","Son jalisciense, ranchera, bolero, huapango"],["Famous songs","Cielito Lindo, El Son de la Negra"],["UNESCO heritage","2011"]],
d:[["1700s–1800s","Mariachi develops in western Mexico"],["1930s","Mariachi spreads through radio and film"],["1994","International Mariachi Festival begins in Guadalajara"],["2011","Added to UNESCO’s heritage list"]],
s2:[["Women in mariachi","All-female mariachi groups, such as Mariachi Reyna de Los Angeles, have grown in popularity since the 1990s."],["Grito","Mariachi performances often include the grito, a joyful shout or yell during the music."]],
g:[["Mariachi","A traditional Mexican music and band"],["Vihuela","A small, high-pitched guitar"],["Guitarrón","A large bass guitar"],["Ranchera","A Mexican song style about love and the countryside"]],
f:["Mariachi comes from western Mexico.","Mariachi bands include trumpets and violins.","Mariachi is on UNESCO’s heritage list."],
fun:"In Mexico City, you can hire a mariachi band on the spot in Plaza Garibaldi."});

ADDART({id:"batik",t:"Batik",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🖼️",w:"Batik",r:["indonesiacountry","tapestry","clothing","silk","cotton","malaysiacountry"],
p:[["Wax-resist dyeing","Wax blocks dye to make patterns on cloth."],["Indonesian heritage","Recognized by UNESCO in 2009."],["Many traditions","Batik-style dyeing is found worldwide."]],
l:"Batik is a technique of decorating cloth by using wax to resist dye. An artist applies hot wax to parts of the fabric in a pattern; when the cloth is dipped in dye, the waxed areas stay their original color. By repeating waxing and dyeing several times with different colors, and finally removing the wax by boiling or scraping, artists create detailed, multicolored designs. Wax-resist dyeing has been practiced for centuries in many parts of the world, including Africa, China, Japan, India and Sri Lanka, but it reached extraordinary refinement on the island of Java in Indonesia, where batik is a central part of culture and identity. Javanese batik makers use a canting, a small copper tool with a spout, to draw fine lines of wax by hand, a process that can take weeks or months for one cloth, called batik tulis; batik cap uses copper stamps for faster production. Patterns carry meanings, and some were once reserved for royalty in the courts of Yogyakarta and Surakarta. In 2009, UNESCO recognized Indonesian batik as Intangible Cultural Heritage, and Indonesia celebrates National Batik Day each 2 October. Batik is also important in Malaysia and elsewhere.",
s:[["The canting","The canting has a small bowl for hot wax and one or more fine spouts. Artists dip it into melted wax and draw on the cloth like a pen, sometimes on both sides of the fabric."],["Patterns and meanings","Famous Javanese patterns include parang, diagonal knife-like waves once worn by royalty, and kawung, circles inspired by palm fruit. Coastal batik from towns such as Pekalongan shows Chinese, Indian and European influences with bright colors."],["Batik around the world","Wax- or starch-resist cloths are made in West Africa, such as Nigerian adire and the colorful wax prints sold across Africa. Japan has rōketsuzome, and the Miao people of China make indigo batik."],["Modern batik","Many Indonesians wear batik shirts at work and formal events, and designers use batik in modern fashion. Machine-printed imitations are common, but handmade batik remains highly valued."]],
k:[["Technique","Wax-resist dyeing"],["Famous center","Java, Indonesia"],["Hand tool","Canting"],["Hand-drawn batik","Batik tulis"],["Stamped batik","Batik cap"],["UNESCO heritage","2009 (Indonesian batik)"]],
d:[["c. 1600s","Batik flourishes in Javanese courts"],["1840s","Copper stamps (cap) introduced for faster production"],["2009","UNESCO recognizes Indonesian batik"],["2009","Indonesia declares National Batik Day (2 October)"]],
s2:[["Crackle effect","When wax cracks during dyeing, thin lines of dye seep in, creating a crackled pattern that many people find beautiful."],["The word","Batik comes from Javanese words often connected with dots or writing."]],
g:[["Batik","Cloth decorated using wax-resist dyeing"],["Resist","A substance that blocks dye"],["Canting","A small spouted tool for applying wax"],["Motif","A repeated design"]],
f:["Batik uses wax to block dye.","Java is famous for batik.","Indonesian batik is on UNESCO’s heritage list."],
fun:"A single hand-drawn batik cloth can take months to finish."});

ADDART({id:"avalanches",t:"Avalanches",c:"Earth Science",i:"🏔️",w:"Avalanche",r:["snow","mountainform","alps","himal","landslides","glaciers"],
p:[["Snow sliding downhill","Masses of snow rushing down mountain slopes."],["Fast and deadly","Some reach over 300 km/h."],["Safety","Forecasts, training and rescue gear save lives."]],
l:"An avalanche is a mass of snow, sometimes mixed with ice, rocks and soil, that suddenly slides or flows rapidly down a mountain slope. Avalanches happen when the snowpack, the layers of snow built up over a winter, becomes unstable and the force of gravity overcomes the strength holding the snow in place. They usually occur on slopes between about 30 and 45 degrees and are often triggered by new heavy snowfall, strong winds piling up snow, rapid warming or rain, or the weight of a skier, snowboarder or snowmobile. In a slab avalanche, the most dangerous kind, a stiff layer of snow breaks away over a weak layer beneath and slides down as a large block that quickly shatters. Loose snow avalanches start at a point and spread out as they fall. Powder avalanches, clouds of snow mixed with air, can reach speeds of more than 300 kilometers per hour. Avalanches kill around 150 people worldwide each year, mostly skiers, climbers and snowmobilers in backcountry areas. Mountain communities protect themselves with forests, fences and barriers, controlled explosions to release snow safely, and avalanche forecasts.",
s:[["Slab avalanches","Most deadly avalanches are slab avalanches, often triggered by the victims themselves. They happen when a weak buried layer, such as frost crystals called surface hoar, collapses under a stronger layer of snow."],["Safety gear","Backcountry travelers carry an avalanche transceiver, which sends and receives signals to locate buried people, a probe and a shovel. Some use airbag backpacks to help stay on the surface."],["Survival","A person buried by an avalanche has the best chance of survival if dug out within about 15 minutes, because snow sets like concrete and air runs out. Rescue dogs can quickly sniff out buried people."],["Protection","Switzerland’s WSL Institute for Snow and Avalanche Research publishes daily avalanche forecasts. Avalanche danger is rated on a five-level European scale from low to very high."]],
k:[["Cause","Unstable snowpack giving way"],["Typical slope angle","About 30–45 degrees"],["Most dangerous type","Slab avalanche"],["Fastest","Powder avalanches, over 300 km/h"],["Deaths per year","About 150 worldwide"],["Safety gear","Transceiver, probe, shovel"]],
d:[["1916","Avalanches during the First World War kill thousands of soldiers in the Alps"],["1970","Huascarán avalanche and landslide in Peru"],["1999","Galtür avalanche in Austria"]],
s2:[["White Friday","On 13 December 1916, avalanches in the Dolomites killed thousands of Austrian and Italian soldiers fighting in the mountains."],["Avalanche dogs","Trained dogs can search an area for buried people much faster than human searchers with probes."]],
g:[["Avalanche","A mass of snow sliding down a slope"],["Snowpack","Layers of snow built up over winter"],["Slab","A stiff layer of snow"],["Transceiver","A device that sends and receives signals"]],
f:["Avalanches often happen on slopes of 30–45 degrees.","Slab avalanches are the most dangerous.","Transceivers help find buried people."],
fun:"Powder avalanches can move faster than a high-speed train."});

ADDART({id:"seamounts",t:"Seamounts",c:"Earth Science",i:"🗻",w:"Seamount",r:["volc","oceancurrents","midoceanridges","coral","atolls","deepsea"],
p:[["Underwater mountains","Volcanic peaks rising from the seafloor."],["Tens of thousands","Most have never been explored."],["Oases of life","They attract fish, corals and sharks."]],
l:"A seamount is an underwater mountain that rises at least 1,000 meters above the surrounding seafloor but does not reach the ocean surface. Most seamounts are extinct volcanoes, formed by eruptions at hotspots, mid-ocean ridges or above subduction zones. Scientists estimate there are more than 100,000 seamounts taller than 1,000 meters, and tens of thousands have been mapped from satellite measurements of tiny bulges in the sea surface caused by their gravity, but only a few hundred have been studied closely. Flat-topped seamounts, called guyots, were once islands whose tops were worn flat by waves before they sank beneath the sea. Seamounts are important ecosystems. They change the flow of ocean currents, bringing nutrients up from the deep, and their slopes provide hard surfaces for cold-water corals, sponges and other animals, so they often host rich communities and attract fish, sharks, whales and seabirds. Many species are found only on particular seamounts. Some seamounts are threatened by deep-sea trawling, which can destroy slow-growing corals, and by proposed mining for minerals in crusts that cover their rocks.",
s:[["Finding seamounts","Satellites measure the height of the sea surface with great accuracy. The extra gravity of a large seamount pulls water toward it, creating a slight bump that reveals its location. Ships with sonar map them in detail."],["Guyots","Guyots were named in 1946 by the geologist Harry Hess after Arnold Guyot. Many in the Pacific were once tropical islands with coral reefs before sinking."],["Seamount chains","Hotspots create chains of seamounts as tectonic plates move over them. The Hawaiian–Emperor seamount chain stretches about 6,000 kilometers across the Pacific."],["Discoveries","Expeditions keep finding new seamounts and species. In 2024, scientists exploring off Chile mapped several previously unknown seamounts, one over 3 kilometers tall, and discovered many new species."]],
k:[["Definition","Underwater mountain at least 1,000 m tall"],["Estimated number","Over 100,000"],["Origin","Mostly extinct volcanoes"],["Flat-topped type","Guyot"],["Famous chain","Hawaiian–Emperor seamounts"],["Threats","Deep-sea trawling and mining"]],
d:[["1946","Harry Hess names guyots"],["1990s","Satellites reveal seamounts worldwide"],["2005","Submarine USS San Francisco hits an uncharted seamount"],["2024","New seamounts mapped off Chile"]],
s2:[["Submarine collision","In 2005, the submarine USS San Francisco crashed into an uncharted seamount in the Pacific at full speed, showing how much of the seafloor was still unmapped."],["Mapping the ocean","The Seabed 2030 project aims to map the entire ocean floor in detail by 2030."]],
g:[["Seamount","An underwater mountain"],["Guyot","A flat-topped seamount"],["Hotspot","A place where hot rock rises from deep in Earth"],["Trawling","Fishing by dragging nets"]],
f:["Seamounts are underwater mountains.","Most seamounts are extinct volcanoes.","Seamounts are rich in life."],
fun:"Satellites can find seamounts by detecting tiny bumps in the sea surface caused by their gravity."});

ADDART({id:"coastalerosion",t:"Cliffs and Coastal Erosion",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌊",w:"Coastal erosion",r:["coasts","rockarches","sealevel","storms","tides"],
p:[["Waves wear away land","The sea constantly reshapes coasts."],["Cliffs, caves and stacks","Erosion creates dramatic landforms."],["Some coasts retreat fast","Up to meters a year in places."]],
l:"Coastal erosion is the wearing away of land along coastlines by waves, tides, currents, wind and storms. Waves are the main force: they hurl water, sand and pebbles against rock, a process called abrasion; trap and compress air in cracks, forcing them apart; and slowly dissolve some rocks, such as limestone. Over time, erosion creates spectacular landforms. Waves undercut the base of rocky coasts, forming notches until the rock above collapses, producing steep cliffs that gradually retreat inland. Where rocks of different strength meet, softer rock erodes into bays while harder rock forms headlands. On headlands, waves attack cracks to form sea caves, which can grow into arches, which collapse to leave isolated pillars called sea stacks, and finally stumps. Famous examples include the Twelve Apostles in Australia, the White Cliffs of Dover in England and the Algarve coast in Portugal. Some coasts made of soft clay or sand retreat by meters each year, threatening homes and roads. Rising sea levels and stronger storms caused by climate change are increasing erosion in many places. People defend coasts with sea walls, rock barriers and groynes, or by restoring dunes, salt marshes and mangroves.",
s:[["From cave to stump","A crack in a headland is widened into a cave. If caves form on both sides, they can meet to make an arch. When the arch roof falls, a sea stack remains, which in turn is worn down to a stump."],["The Twelve Apostles","Along Australia’s Great Ocean Road, limestone stacks called the Twelve Apostles rise from the sea. Erosion continues: one collapsed in 2005, leaving fewer standing."],["Fast-eroding coasts","Parts of England’s Holderness coast, made of soft glacial clay, retreat by about 2 meters a year, one of the fastest rates in Europe. Villages there have disappeared into the sea over centuries."],["Defending coasts","Hard defenses, such as sea walls, can protect towns but may increase erosion nearby. Soft approaches, such as adding sand to beaches and restoring dunes and wetlands, work with natural processes."]],
k:[["Main force","Waves"],["Processes","Abrasion, hydraulic action, solution"],["Landforms","Cliffs, caves, arches, stacks, stumps"],["Famous stacks","Twelve Apostles, Australia"],["Fast-eroding coast","Holderness, England (about 2 m a year)"],["Growing threat","Sea-level rise and storms"]],
d:[["1362","Storms destroy the town of Rungholt on the North Sea coast"],["2005","One of the Twelve Apostles collapses"],["2017","Malta’s Azure Window collapses"]],
s2:[["Lost towns","Dunwich in England was once a busy medieval port; most of it has been lost to the sea since the 1200s."],["Sand on the move","Longshore drift, waves moving sand along a coast, can build spits and sandbars in one place while eroding beaches elsewhere."]],
g:[["Erosion","The wearing away of land"],["Abrasion","Wearing away by rubbing with sand and stones"],["Sea stack","A pillar of rock standing in the sea"],["Headland","A point of land sticking out into the sea"]],
f:["Waves are the main cause of coastal erosion.","Sea caves can become arches and stacks.","Some coasts retreat meters each year."],
fun:"The medieval port of Dunwich in England has almost completely vanished into the sea."});

ADDART({id:"hail",t:"Hail",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Hail",r:["thunderstorms","snow","clouds","storms","weatherforecast","climate"],
p:[["Balls of ice from the sky","Hailstones form inside thunderstorms."],["Layers like an onion","They grow as they are tossed up and down."],["Damaging","Big hail smashes cars, roofs and crops."]],
l:"Hail is precipitation in the form of balls or lumps of ice, called hailstones, that fall from thunderstorms. Hailstones form inside tall cumulonimbus storm clouds with strong upward winds, called updrafts. Tiny ice particles or frozen raindrops are carried high into the cloud, where temperatures are far below freezing and supercooled water droplets, liquid water colder than 0 °C, freeze onto them. The growing stones may be carried up and down several times, adding layers of ice, until they become too heavy for the updraft to hold and fall to the ground. Cutting a hailstone open often reveals rings like an onion. Most hailstones are pea-sized, under about 1 centimeter, but in severe thunderstorms they can grow as big as golf balls, baseballs or even larger. The largest hailstone recorded in the United States, found in Vivian, South Dakota, in 2010, measured about 20 centimeters across and weighed nearly 0.9 kilograms. Hail causes billions of dollars of damage each year to cars, roofs, windows and crops, and can injure people and animals. Hail is most common in summer and in regions such as the US Great Plains, Argentina, northern India, South Africa and parts of Europe and China.",
s:[["How hail grows","Hailstones grow by collecting supercooled droplets. Clear layers form when water freezes slowly, and cloudy layers when it freezes quickly, trapping air bubbles. The stronger the updraft, the larger the hail can grow."],["Hail Alley","The area where Colorado, Nebraska and Wyoming meet in the United States, known as Hail Alley, gets more hail than almost anywhere in North America. Parts of Argentina and Bangladesh also see severe hail."],["Damage","A 2023 hailstorm in northern Italy produced hailstones about 19 centimeters across, among the largest in Europe. Hail is a leading cause of insurance claims for cars and homes in many countries."],["Hail or not?","Small, soft pellets called graupel or snow pellets fall in winter and are not true hail. Sleet is rain that freezes into small ice pellets before reaching the ground."]],
k:[["Forms in","Thunderstorms (cumulonimbus clouds)"],["Needs","Strong updrafts and supercooled water"],["Typical size","Pea-sized (under 1 cm)"],["US record","About 20 cm, Vivian, South Dakota (2010)"],["Hail Alley","Colorado, Nebraska, Wyoming"],["Damage","Cars, roofs, crops"]],
d:[["1888","Deadly hailstorm in Moradabad, India"],["2010","Record US hailstone falls in South Dakota"],["2023","Giant hail in northern Italy"]],
s2:[["Onion rings","Scientists study the layers inside hailstones to learn about conditions inside storms."],["Hail cannons","Some farmers use ‘hail cannons’ that fire loud blasts at storms to try to stop hail, but there is no scientific evidence that they work."]],
g:[["Hailstone","A ball or lump of ice falling from a storm"],["Updraft","A rising current of air"],["Supercooled water","Liquid water below 0 °C"],["Cumulonimbus","A tall thunderstorm cloud"]],
f:["Hail forms in thunderstorms.","Hailstones grow in layers.","Large hail can damage cars and crops."],
fun:"The largest hailstone recorded in the United States was about the size of a volleyball."});

ADDART({id:"blizzards",t:"Blizzards",c:"Earth Science",i:"🌨️",w:"Blizzard",r:["snow","storms","hail","weatherforecast","extremeheat","arctic"],
p:[["Snowstorms with strong winds","Blowing snow reduces visibility."],["Whiteout","People cannot see the ground or horizon."],["Dangerous cold","Blizzards can bring frostbite and hypothermia."]],
l:"A blizzard is a severe winter storm with strong winds and falling or blowing snow that sharply reduces visibility, often to near zero. In the United States, the National Weather Service defines a blizzard as winds of at least 56 kilometers per hour with snow reducing visibility to less than 400 meters for at least three hours. A blizzard does not need heavy snowfall: strong winds lifting snow already on the ground can create a ‘ground blizzard’. In a whiteout, swirling snow and white skies make it impossible to tell where the ground ends and the sky begins, so people can easily become lost even near their homes. Blizzards also bring dangerous cold, as wind makes temperatures feel much lower, called wind chill, which can cause frostbite and hypothermia. Drifting snow can bury cars, roads and railways, cut power and trap people for days. Blizzards are common in the Great Plains of North America, the Canadian prairies, Russia, Mongolia, northern China, Scandinavia and Antarctica, where winds blowing off the ice sheet can create fierce, long-lasting blizzards. Modern forecasting gives warnings days in advance, helping people prepare.",
s:[["Famous blizzards","The Great Blizzard of 1888 buried the northeastern United States under more than a meter of snow, with drifts up to 15 meters, killing about 400 people. The 1972 Iran blizzard, one of the deadliest, buried villages and killed about 4,000 people."],["Whiteouts","In whiteouts, people lose their sense of direction. Pilots, drivers and hikers are advised to stop and shelter rather than keep moving."],["Wind chill","Wind removes heat from skin faster, so a temperature of −10 °C with strong winds can feel like −25 °C or colder, causing frostbite in minutes."],["Dzud","In Mongolia, severe winters called dzud, often with blizzards and extreme cold, can kill millions of livestock, devastating herding families."]],
k:[["Definition (US)","Winds 56+ km/h, visibility under 400 m, 3+ hours"],["Key danger","Whiteouts and extreme cold"],["Ground blizzard","Wind lifting fallen snow"],["Wind chill","Makes it feel colder"],["Common in","Great Plains, Russia, Mongolia, Antarctica"],["Deadliest","1972 Iran blizzard"]],
d:[["1888","Great Blizzard of 1888 in the northeastern US"],["1972","Iran blizzard"],["1978","Great Blizzard in the US Midwest and Northeast"],["1993","‘Storm of the Century’ in eastern North America"]],
s2:[["Stay put","Experts advise people caught in a blizzard in a car to stay inside, keep the exhaust pipe clear of snow and run the engine only occasionally for warmth."],["Antarctic winds","At Commonwealth Bay in Antarctica, known as ‘the home of the blizzard’, winds average about 70 km/h over a year."]],
g:[["Blizzard","A severe snowstorm with strong winds"],["Whiteout","When snow makes it impossible to see"],["Wind chill","How cold it feels with wind"],["Frostbite","Freezing of skin and tissue"]],
f:["Blizzards have strong winds and blowing snow.","Whiteouts make it hard to see.","Wind chill makes it feel colder."],
fun:"A blizzard can happen without any new snow falling, just from wind blowing old snow around."});

ADDART({id:"groundwater",t:"Groundwater and Aquifers",c:"Earth Science",i:"💧",w:"Groundwater",r:["wcycle","oases","sanitation","sinkholes","droughts"],
p:[["Hidden water","Water stored underground in soil and rock."],["Aquifers","Layers of rock that hold and release water."],["Drinking water","Billions of people depend on it."]],
l:"Groundwater is water that fills the tiny spaces between particles of soil and sand and the cracks in rocks beneath the ground. It comes from rain and melting snow that soak into the ground and slowly seep downward until they reach a zone where all the spaces are filled with water, called the saturated zone; its top surface is the water table. Layers of rock or sediment that hold enough water and let it flow, such as sand, gravel, sandstone and cracked limestone, are called aquifers. Groundwater moves slowly, sometimes only centimeters a day, and can stay underground for days or thousands of years. It is the largest store of liquid fresh water on Earth, far more than in all rivers and lakes, and supplies drinking water to about half the world’s people and much of the water used for farming. People reach it through wells and springs. In many regions, however, groundwater is being pumped out faster than rain can refill it, causing water tables to fall, wells to dry up, land to sink and, near coasts, seawater to seep in. Pollution from farms, factories and landfills can also contaminate aquifers, and cleaning them is very difficult.",
s:[["Famous aquifers","The Ogallala Aquifer under the US Great Plains waters a large share of American farmland but is being depleted. The Nubian Sandstone Aquifer under Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad holds ancient ‘fossil’ water from wetter times thousands of years ago."],["Springs and wells","Springs form where the water table meets the surface. Wells are dug or drilled below the water table. Artesian wells flow without pumping because the water is under pressure."],["Sinking land","When too much groundwater is pumped, the ground can sink, a process called subsidence. Parts of Mexico City, Jakarta and California’s Central Valley have sunk several meters."],["Protecting groundwater","Managing pumping, recharging aquifers with stored rainwater, limiting pollution and monitoring water levels help keep groundwater available for the future."]],
k:[["Definition","Water stored underground"],["Top of saturated zone","Water table"],["Water-bearing layer","Aquifer"],["Share of liquid fresh water","The largest store on Earth"],["People relying on it","About half the world for drinking"],["Threats","Over-pumping and pollution"]],
d:[["1950s","Large-scale pumping of the Ogallala Aquifer begins"],["2022","UN World Water Development Report focuses on groundwater"]],
s2:[["Old water","Some groundwater in deep aquifers has been underground for more than a million years."],["Caves","In limestone areas, groundwater dissolves rock to create caves and underground rivers."]],
g:[["Groundwater","Water in soil and rocks underground"],["Aquifer","Rock or sediment that holds and transmits water"],["Water table","The top of the saturated zone"],["Subsidence","Sinking of the ground"]],
f:["Groundwater is stored in aquifers.","About half the world drinks groundwater.","Over-pumping can make land sink."],
fun:"Some groundwater deep underground has been there for more than a million years."});
